# CONV xdg - the XDG Base Directory Specification, version 0.8
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Tier CONVENTION: a published specification exists, but it is a
# freedesktop.org agreement among desktop projects, not a formal standard
# that a system claims. A failure is reported INSPECT for a person to judge.
# Source: basedir.html (section numbers below are that page's).
#
# Deliberately not asserted (application duties, no system tooling to run):
#  - relative paths in the XDG_* variables are invalid and must be ignored
#  - $XDG_*_HOME / $XDG_*_DIRS precedence and the search order
#  - $HOME/.local/bin appearing in PATH (a distribution duty, environment)
#  - XDG_RUNTIME_DIR lifetime (login/logout, reboot), "local file system",
#    "file change notifications", the periodic clean-up rules: not observable
#    from a program with the interfaces every system has

assertion  CONV.xdg.001
anchor     basedir.html#3/XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
class      disputed
says       When XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is set in the environment the suite was started
           from, it is an absolute path naming an existing directory owned by
           the current user whose mode is exactly 0700.
test       t/001-runtime-dir.c
note       tier CONVENTION; source basedir.html section 3, uses MUST. A system
           that sets the variable for a session is held to it. NOTINUSE when
           the variable is unset (the specification then only asks an
           application for a fallback). conv/run.sh passes the caller's value
           as PC_CONV_XDG_RUNTIME_DIR. Sources do not disagree.
nomutant   reads the caller's environment; checked with a fixture directory of
           the wrong mode (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.xdg.002
anchor     basedir.html#3/XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
class      disputed
says       The directory named by XDG_RUNTIME_DIR supports symbolic links, hard
           links with a correct link count, access modes, record locking seen
           across processes, sparse files, shared memory mappings, AF_UNIX
           sockets bound to a name in it, and file names made of any bytes
           except slash and NUL.
test       t/002-runtime-features.c
note       tier CONVENTION; source basedir.html section 3 (the list of
           features the directory must be fully featured for). File change
           notifications are not tested (no portable interface). NOTINUSE when
           the variable is unset. Sources do not disagree.

assertion  CONV.xdg.003
anchor     basedir.html#3/XDG_DATA_HOME
anchor     basedir.html#3/XDG_CONFIG_HOME
anchor     basedir.html#3/XDG_STATE_HOME
anchor     basedir.html#3/XDG_CACHE_HOME
anchor     basedir.html#4
class      disputed
says       With the XDG variables unset, a program can create the default
           per-user directories under HOME (config, local data, state, cache)
           with mode 0700, can create files in them, and a directory that
           already exists keeps its mode.
test       t/003-default-dirs.c
note       tier CONVENTION; source basedir.html sections 3 and 4 (defaults and
           the 0700 creation rule). Runs in the test's private HOME, so it
           checks that the system lets a program do what the specification
           asks, not any user's real home. Sources do not disagree.

assertion  CONV.xdg.004
anchor     basedir.html#3/XDG_DATA_DIRS
anchor     basedir.html#3/XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
class      disputed
says       The data directory that the specification's default search path ends
           with, /usr/share, exists and is a directory; the other default
           search directories, /usr/local/share and /etc/xdg, are directories
           wherever they exist.
test       t/004-default-search-dirs.c
note       tier CONVENTION; source basedir.html section 3 (the defaults). The
           specification does not require the other two to exist. Sources do
           not disagree.
nomutant   file-system check; fixture tree via PC_CONV_ROOT (conv/neg/run.sh)
